# `distr::Lomax`

The Lomax (Pareto type II) distribution: a power-law distribution that starts at zero, the standard heavy-tailed model of lifetimes and claim sizes.

## Signature

```cpp
pluss::Series distr::pdf(dst::Lomax, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::logpdf(dst::Lomax, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::cdf(dst::Lomax, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::logcdf(dst::Lomax, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::sample(dst::Lomax, const std::vector<double>& params, std::initializer_list<std::int64_t> sizes, pluss::Config cfg = {}, std::uint64_t seed = 0);
```

## What It Does

The Lomax distribution is a Pareto distribution shifted to start at zero: its density is `(alpha/scale)(1 + x/scale)^(-(alpha + 1))`, so the probability of exceeding `x` is `(1 + x/scale)^(-alpha)`. It is a gamma mixture of exponential distributions, which makes it a model for a population whose rates differ, and a standard heavy-tailed claim-size distribution. The mean exists only for `alpha > 1`.

The kind-based functions take `{scale, alpha}`.

The parameters, in the order of the `params` list:

| Parameter | Constraint | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `scale` | > 0 | the scale of the axis |
| `alpha` | > 0 | the tail index: the probability of exceeding `x` decays like `x^(-alpha)` |

Support: `x >= 0`.

## Parameters

`const pluss::Series& x`
: The points at which to evaluate, a `Series` of any shape; the result has the same shape. Points outside the support have density zero and distribution value 0 or 1.

`const std::vector<double>& params`
: The parameters of the family, in the order shown in the table above. A list of the wrong length is refused.

`std::initializer_list<std::int64_t> sizes`
: The shape of the array of random draws, for example `{n}` or `{rows, columns}`.

`pluss::Config cfg`
: The dtype and platform of the draws; the default is Float32 on the CPU, so request Float64 when precision matters.

`std::uint64_t seed`
: The seed of the stream. The same seed always gives the same draws; a seed of 0 is a fixed default, not a random one.

## Returns

`pdf`, `logpdf`, `cdf` and `logcdf` return a `Series` of the shape of `x`; `sample` returns a `Series` of the requested shape. 

## Errors

- Parameters outside their valid range are refused with an error that names the requirement (`scale` and `alpha` must be positive).
- A `params` list of the wrong length is refused.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Example 1 evaluates the density (mass) function, the distribution function and the log-density of the Lomax on a small grid with `distr::pdf`, `distr::cdf` and `distr::logpdf`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(5);

  const auto x = pluss::make_series({0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0, 15.0}, f64);
  const std::vector<double> params = {1.5, 3.0};

  std::cout << "x:";
  for (double v : x.host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "pdf:";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::Lomax, x, params).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "cdf:";
  for (double v : distr::cdf(dst::Lomax, x, params).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "logpdf:";
  for (double v : distr::logpdf(dst::Lomax, x, params).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
}
```

The density is largest on this grid at x = 0, where it is 2; the distribution function runs from 0 at x = 0 to 0.99925 at x = 15, and it is the running total of the density. `logpdf` is the logarithm of the density, for example log(2) = 0.69315 at x = 0.

### Example 2

Example 2 checks that the three descriptions of the same probability agree: the integral of the density (sum of the masses) over a range, the difference of the distribution function at its ends, and the fraction of random draws that land in it.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(5);
  const std::vector<double> params = {1.5, 3.0};

  // The three ways to the probability that the variable lies in [0.5, 6]:
  const auto grid = pluss::series::linspace(0.5, 6.0, 3001, f64);
  const double area = distr::pdf(dst::Lomax, grid, params).antidiff(grid).trapezoid().item<double>();

  const auto edges = pluss::make_series({0.5, 6.0}, f64);
  const auto c = distr::cdf(dst::Lomax, edges, params).host<double>();

  const auto draws = distr::sample(dst::Lomax, params, {200000}, f64, 7ULL);
  const double fraction = ((draws >= 0.5).to(pluss::ScalarType::Float64) * (draws <= 6.0).to(pluss::ScalarType::Float64)).mean().item<double>();

  std::cout << "integral of the density: " << area << '\n';
  std::cout << "cdf(6) - cdf(0.5): " << c[1] - c[0] << '\n';
  std::cout << "fraction of 200000 draws in range: " << fraction << '\n';
}
```

The integral of the density (0.41388) and the difference of the distribution function (0.41387) agree to 1.00e-05 in absolute value: they are two computations of one probability. The fraction of random draws (0.41647) differs from it by 0.0026, which is the size of the sampling error of a fraction of 200000 draws (a standard error of about 0.0011), so the density, the distribution function and the sampler describe the same distribution.

### Example 3

Example 3 shows how the shape changes with the parameters: the density at the same points for 3 parameter settings.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(4);
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 4.0, 8.0}, f64);

  std::cout << "x:";
  for (double v : x.host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "alpha 1.5:";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::Lomax, x, {1.5, 1.5}).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "alpha 3  :";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::Lomax, x, {1.5, 3.0}).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "alpha 8  :";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::Lomax, x, {1.5, 8.0}).host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
}
```

Read down the rows: `alpha 1.5` peaks on this grid at x = 0 (value 1); `alpha 3` peaks on this grid at x = 0 (value 2); `alpha 8` peaks on this grid at x = 0 (value 5.3333). The parameters move the location, the width and the shape of the curve, which is what choosing a member of the family means.

### Example 4

Example 4 checks the distribution function against its closed form, `1 - (1 + x/scale)^(-alpha)`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(6);
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0, 15.0}, f64);
  const std::vector<double> params = {1.5, 3.0};

  const auto formula = 1.0 - (x * (1.0 / 1.5) + 1.0).pow(-3.0);
  const auto library = distr::cdf(dst::Lomax, x, params);

  std::cout << "x:";
  for (double v : x.host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "formula:";
  for (double v : formula.host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "distr::cdf:";
  for (double v : library.host<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << std::scientific << std::setprecision(2) << "largest difference: " << (formula - library).abs().max().item<double>() << '\n';
}
```

The formula and `distr::cdf` agree to 1.11e-16 over the grid, so the library implements exactly the closed form quoted above; the same expression is the quickest way to check a parameterisation before relying on it.

